-- title:
--   A universal primitive ℓ-th root in the fine moduli ring
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime with $q \ge 5$, let $M'$ be a nonzero natural number not divisible by $q$, and let $\ell$ be a prime with $\ell \ge 3$, $\ell \ne q$ and $\ell \nmid M'$. Let $A_0$ be a discrete valuation domain whose maximal ideal is generated by $q$, containing a primitive $\ell$-th root of unity, and in which $\ell$ and $M'$ are units. Assume, as the hypotheses `hℓ` and `hM`, that over every $A_0$-algebra the predicate [`ModularCurve.IsLevelPStructure`](def/ModularCurve_KatzLevelP.html#L104) for $\ell$ is preserved by the action of a Weierstrass variable change $C$ on `LevelPData` (the quadruple $(x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q)$ transformed by $C$), and that [`ModularCurve.IsGamma0PowAt`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma0Pow.html#L55) for $(p,k)$ is preserved by the corresponding transformation `kernelVariableChangeDeg` of monic kernel polynomials of degree `gamma0PowDeg p k`. Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a family of relative group laws on the projective Weierstrass models over $A_0$-algebras with unit discriminant, satisfying `IsChordTangent` (each law is computed by addition on points over fields) and `IsOriginIdentity` (its unit section is the origin chart section killing $x/y$ and $z/y$), and let $\mathcal{T}$ be a transport structure for raw Drinfeld pairs of level $q$ satisfying `IsSectionTransport`, so that its variable-change and coefficient-change operations are compatible with the induced maps of projective models. Assume further (`hVC`, `hCO`) that every Weierstrass variable change, and every $A_0$-algebra map applied to coefficients, is realised by a graded ring homomorphism of the graded quotient rings `projModelGradingCR` satisfying `IsVariableChangeHom`, respectively `IsCoefficientHom`, and carrying the irrelevant ideal appropriately. Finally let $P_0$ be an abstract fine moduli package for the moduli datum of `rigidDataPow A₀ ℓ M' q hℓ hM 𝒢 𝒯`, the rigidification of the product of the $\Gamma_0(M')$ component (tuples of kernel polynomials indexed by the prime factors of $M'$), the Katz level-$\ell$ component and the Drinfeld level-$q$ component: thus $P_0$ consists of a commutative $A_0$-algebra $B_0$ together with a point `univ` over $B_0$ such that every point of the datum over an $A_0$-algebra $T$ is $\mathrm{map}\,\varphi(\mathrm{univ})$ for a unique $A_0$-algebra map $\varphi : B_0 \to T$. The conclusion is that there is an element $\xi_B \in B_0$ with the following three properties: the $\ell$-th cyclotomic polynomial over $A_0$ vanishes at $\xi_B$; for every $a \in A_0$ with $a^\ell = 1$ and $a \ne 1$ the quotient $B_0/(\xi_B - a)$ is nontrivial; and for every field $F$ which is an $A_0$-algebra and every point $y$ of the datum over $F$ with $\ell \ne 0$ in $F$, the image of $\xi_B$ under the classifying map $P_0.\mathrm{classify}\,y : B_0 \to F$ is a primitive $\ell$-th root of unity.
--
--   The element $\xi_B$ is the universal value of the Weil pairing $e_\ell(P,Q)$ of the level-$\ell$ basis carried by the universal object of the rigidified $\Gamma_0(M') \times \Gamma(\ell) \times \Gamma(q)^{\mathrm{Drinfeld}}$ moduli problem, together with the statement that each of the $\ell - 1$ possible primitive values is actually attained on the fine moduli ring $B_0$. It is used in the analysis of the minimal primes of the $\Gamma_0$-type moduli ring in the unramified setting, where the geometric components of the full-level curve are separated according to the Weil-pairing constant.
